\begin_layout Section
The law of definite proportions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The law of definite proportions,
 also known law of definite composition,
 states that regardless of the amount,
 a pure compound always contains the same elements in the same proportions by mass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
History
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although many experimenters had long assumed the truth of the principle in general,
 the French chemist Joseph-Louis Proust first accumulated conclusive evidence for it in a series of researches on the composition of many substances,
 especially the oxides of iron (1797).
 Another French chemist,
 Claude Berthollet,
 who held for indefinite proportions,
 contested Proust’s findings,
 but the Scottish chemist Thomas Thomson confirmed some of them.
 Proust had definitely proved “metals are not capable of indefinite degrees of oxidation.” The principle was then concretely formulated by the English chemist John Dalton in his chemical atomic theory (1808).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Examples of the Law of Definite Proportions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us take,
 for example,
 the compound water.
 Whatever the source of water,
 its composition is that of two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Law of Definite Proportions:
 % by Weight of Hydrogen and Oxygen in Water This figure shows that water,
 from any source,
 is always made up of two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygen.
 If we calculate the molecular weight of water,
 we come up with 18 g/mol.
 In 1 mole of water,
 there are 2 grams of hydrogen and 16 grams of oxygen.
 By weight,
 we have a percentage of 11% hydrogen and 89% oxygen in one mole of water.
 This translates into a ratio of 1:8 of hydrogen to oxygen in water.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Images/CC/Ch/ImagesReduced/resized_screen_shot_2014-09-07_at_2.28.55_pm.jpg
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Law of Definite Proportions illustrates that whatever the amount of water,
 whether it be 2 moles or 54 grams,
 the ratio of the amount of hydrogen to oxygen by weight will always be the same,
 just like the egg:sugar:butter ratio in chocolate cake should always be the same to maintain consistent taste.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's look at another compound made up of three atoms this time,
 such as Ethyl Alcohol,
 which consists of 2 atoms of Carbon,
 6 atoms of Hydrogen and one atom of Oxygen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to the figure,
 we can calculate the percentage of each element in ethyl alcohol by weight,
 and we come up with 52% carbon,
 13% hydrogen and 35% oxygen by weight.
 Based on the Law of Definite Proportions,
 the percentages of carbon,
 hydrogen and oxygen in ethyl alcohol,
 will always be the same,
 no matter what the amount.
\end_layout

